t yes-or-no answer you know I know  that may be satisfying but there's so  much in the air I know did you advise  any of the people who are in the book  prior to publication albeit under  different names that you were doing this  for sure I did yes and I got a mixed I  got a mixed response the union is not  thrilled about the fact that I'm writing  about saltine and this is despite the  fact that every major company you know  not only knows about saltine but has  ways to actively look for salt and  spends a lot of money to do that but I  understand they didn't want the Union is  at a point where they really want to  take care of their messaging and this  was not vetted by the Union in any way  and other people were excited about it  within the Union to have this story told  so it was a mixed response  yeah just just a couple more questions  okay if we have any more no all right oh  sure yes Kaleo so yes Kalia was this so  it's very hard to find it's a lot harder  than you think to find the leader that  you want to join your committee because  too often you get caught up in oh this  person has a lot of people congregated  around them all the time but that's  because they're funny it's not because  they're this person is a leader or this  person is you know really nurturing but  maybe it's because you know he he's a  great dad not necessarily because he's a  leader  I knew Kalia was a leader because she is  somebody who her father was a drug  dealer who had been killed her mother  was a drug addict and she had grown up  taking care of her little brother moving  him around the country  and so she was tough right  and then she one day at work she had you  know a lot of people around her who  looked up to her and one day at work we  had to do at the buffet we had to clean  down the tables and then have management  come over and sign off and say yes this  person's table is clean and I of course  never did that and as like the one white  guy always got away with just being like  yep my table is good I'm just gonna  leave but they still had to you know  have people sign off and the manager  gave her a really hard time about  cleaning off this table and she just  said you know what  Randy why are you giving me a hard time  right now and Randy the manager just  kind of like shriveled and said if you  want to push back come to my office and  we could have this conversation and  Kalia said nope I'm gonna do it right  here in front of all my co-workers where  I have witnesses and Randy just walked  away and that was the last time Randy  ever pushed Clea around so I knew she  was definitely a leader we wanted and  she when I left the first casino I  worked at it was the hardest for me to  like say goodbye to her because she was  the first person on our committee  she stayed through  you know a full year of work and she was  kind of disappointed because I think she  saw me as sort of this Patriot in the  buffet despite the fact that she didn't  know I was working with the Union so I  heard that yes she was eventually fired  because she accrued too many small  little write-ups which is another way to  get workers and get this turnover so I  heard that she was fired but I you know  I think it's time that I send her a copy  of the book and see what yeah yeah as  you're describing this it strikes me  that how long will you do this two years  the dumbest woman oh crazily lonely two  years where no one around you got to  know like what you were doing while you  were doing it who you were what you  represented and when you talked about  kind of doing it again is that on a  personal level is that not a huge  personal sacrifice that like is that for  you worth it because that sounds really  sad I wouldn't describe it as lonely  it's more like having a secret because I  wasn't undercover in the sense that I  was always myself I didn't have some  other identity 99.9% of my conversations  weren't about the Union or anything they  were just hanging out with friends and  getting to know friends  so of course carrying that secret is  exhausting  and can take its toll but there's no  part of me that felt lonely plus it was  in Miami so people were visiting all the  time so I had friends that were always  coming to visit but I genuinely liked my  co-workers and the other organizers I  was working with and could be myself in  front of them in just about every way  except for the whole journalism thing so  thank you so much  [Applause]  here's a look at some authors recently  featured on book TVs afterwards our  weekly author interview program former  Bush administration official John you  argued that executive power has gone  beyond its constitutional limits under  President Obama Michael Eric Dyson  explored how race has impacted the Obama  administration in Washington Post  columnist EJ Dionne  argued that the Republican Party's  adoption of senator Barry Goldwater's  conservative principles is driving away  moderate voters
 

